  ],
  "hints": [
    "starting with the widest possible container (both ends), what determines how much water it holds? the shorter of the two lines.",
    "if you move the pointer at the TALLER line inward, can the area ever improve? think about what happens to both width and the limiting height.",
    "moving the shorter line's pointer inward is the only move that could possibly find something better, since width can only shrink",
    "track the max area seen as you move the shorter pointer inward each step, until the pointers meet"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "moving both pointers every step instead of only the one at the shorter line",
    "forgetting that area uses the min of the two heights, not the max or the sum",
    "off-by-one on the width calculation (j - i, not j - i + 1, since width is the gap between lines)"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of heights, `height`, one per vertical line, all\nstanding on the x-axis. Any two lines, together with the x-axis, form a\ncontainer.\n\n**Find the two lines that hold the most water, and return how much that container can hold.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "2 <= height.length <= 10^5",
      "0 <= height[i] <= 10^4"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "only two lines in the array",
      "heights that increase, decrease, or are all equal",
      "the tallest lines are right next to each other (small width, but tall)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
